<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>If we have two vertical lines, <code>heights[i]</code> and <code>heights=
[j]</code>, the amount of water that can be contained between these two lin=
es is <code>min(heights[i], heights[j]) * (j - i)</code>, where <code>j - i=
</code> represents the width of the container. We take the minimum height b=
ecause filling water above this height would result in overflow.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"Image represents a graphical illustration of a calculation along=
side its formula and result.  The left side shows a 2D Cartesian coordinate=
 system with a light-blue rectangle representing a volume of water. The rec=
tangle's base extends from x=3D0 to x=3D2 on the x-axis and its height is 3=
 units along the y-axis, reaching from y=3D0 to y=3D3.  A vertical line seg=
ment at x=3D1 is also present within the rectangle.  A curved grey arrow or=
iginates from the top-right corner of the rectangle and points to the right=
, indicating the direction of the calculation's result. To the right of the=
 graph, the formula 'water =3D min(4, 3) =E2=8B=85 (2 - 0)' is displayed, c=
alculating the volume of water.  'min(4, 3)' finds the minimum value betwee=
n 4 and 3 (which is 3), and this is multiplied by the base length (2 - 0 =
=3D 2). The subsequent lines show the calculation steps: '3 =E2=8B=85 2' an=
d the final result '6', representing the total volume of water (likely in s=
ome arbitrary units)." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"388" height=3D"143" decodi=
ng=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" style=3D"color:transparent" src=3D"https://byt=
ebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/two-pointers/largest-container/i=
mage-01-04-1-T7WVY3OS.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div></f=
igure>
<p>In other words, the area of the container depends on two things:</p>
<ul>
<li>The <strong>width</strong> of the rectangle.</li>
<li>The <strong>height</strong> of the rectangle, as dictated by the shorte=
r of the two lines.</li>
</ul>
<p>The brute force approach to this problem involves checking all pairs of =
lines, and returning the largest area found between each pair:</p>
